<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Savva Morozov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1862">1862</a>–<a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Savva Timofeyevich Morozov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Savva Timofeyevich Morozov was a Russian textile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> and philanthropist of an Old Believer merchant family, the fifth-richest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> at the beginning of the 20th century. He studied physics and mathematics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> University and chemistry at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, where he studied the British textile industry. As the largest shareholder of the Moscow Art <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> he funded the improvement of its building on Kamergersky Lane in 1902 and its electrification in 1903. Influenced by Maxim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Gorky</a>, he funded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, including the newspaper Iskra. He died of a gunshot wound in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannes">Cannes</a> in 1905; his death was ruled a suicide, though various murder theories exist.</p></div>
